42:### 2.1 Definition (per sample, on the metadata dict produced by `AR_md.py`)
60:**Exactness statement.** For any integer roll `d`, `canon(rot_d(md))` and `canon(md)` have the *identical recovered-depth image* `D_canon` (bitwise; the hash of §5's Tier 1) and vector/pose values equal up to the composition `R(dj')·R(d)` vs `R(dj)` of float32 rotation matrices (≤ ~1e-6 relative). bf16 conditioning autocast then rounds most of that away; the residual is *measured* (`ε_cond`, Tier 2), never assumed. (A bitwise variant — rebuild the point cloud from `D` — is possible for `depth` but not for the poses and is NOT proposed.) Naming (review N10): in tie cases 2–3 this is a *joint geometry-plus-query canonicalizer*; the record carries the tie class so those items are identifiable. Cost (review N11): one `[3,256,512]` division/max/argmax + one roll/rotation per sample; measured latency is reported from the smoke run and the eval logs, not asserted as zero.
64:### 2.2 Where it is applied
70:  - **Protocol binding, fail-closed (review B2, r2 H1):** `resolve_canonicalize_protocol(flag, file_cfg, embedded_cfg, allow_mismatch)` runs BEFORE model construction, on every evaluation. Canonicalization is *in play* when **any** of: the checkpoint's embedded `training` block contains a `canonicalize` key (any value), the file config contains one, `--canonicalize` is given, or `--allow-canon-mismatch` is given — so a CANON checkpoint paired with a P1 config and no flags is caught (the embedded block is read first; a checkpoint without `model_config` is fatal whenever the file config or a flag puts canonicalization in play, and a checkpoint with an embedded `canonicalize` block is in play by itself). Then: the embedded and file `training.canonicalize` blocks are compared **type-strictly** (absent ≠ `{enabled:false}` ≠ `{enabled:true}`; `1` ≠ `true`); disagreement is fatal and is NOT what `--allow-canon-mismatch` authorizes — that flag only permits `trained ≠ applied` when embedded == file, and passing it when `trained == applied` is itself fatal (an unnecessary allow flag must not silently record an ordinary cell). Outside "in play" the function returns the legacy triple and the run is byte-identical to today. Record fields: `canonicalize_trained`, `canonicalize_applied`, `canonicalize_mismatch`, `canonicalize_img_w`, `canonicalize_tiebreak`, `canonicalize_tie_counts` (dict), `model_config_sha256_file`, `model_config_sha256_embedded`. Filenames: `_canon` when applied, `_canonmis` when a mismatch was authorized (so trained-but-not-applied cells are also distinguishable), placed after the method suffix and before the rotation token; absent flags ⇒ names and records **byte-identical** to today (tested on fixed inputs; injectivity tested across canonical/non-canonical × mismatch × fixed/random × vanilla/FA × ARE for both metrics and predictions names).
